  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 251 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nRidging across the Western United States alongside a shortwave \ntrough digging southeastward across the Midwest is forecast to \ncreate northwesterly flow at 500-mb overhead Friday morning, if \nit is not already in place. This flow pattern would promote a \nmodest 60- 80 kt jet streak from the Pacific Northwest into the \nNorthern Rockies. Surface winds out of the northwest are likely \nin the afternoon in association with a surface high in the \nIntermountain West and a surface low in South-Central Canada, \nboth favorably located with respect to the upper pattern. These \ndownsloping winds support highs climbing on Friday, with the \ncurrent forecast in the upper 50s and lower 60s. NBM guidance \nsuggests that these northwesterly winds could gust into the \n20-30 MPH range north of I-70 during the late morning and early \nafternoon hours, however overestimation biases would hint at the\nlower end of this range. Even so, relative humidities (RH) are \nnot expected to drop below 15% based on LREF probabilities, \nwhich would keep the risk for fire weather conditions to a \nminimum. \n\nGuidance indicates a shortwave trough embedded in the \nnorthwesterly flow regime may impact the area late Friday night \nand Saturday, though it is currently uncertain whether this \nfeature will be present, and if so, how strong it will be. When \nlooking at LREF contributors, ENS members seem to show this \nfeature most consistently, though the bottom 10% of GEFS and \nGEPS members also show the shortwave. If this shortwave \ndevelops, precipitation appears likely. When looking at the LREF\nGrand Ensemble (all members), we see a 20-30% chance for \ngreater than 0.1 inches of snowfall in portions of Eastern \nColorado Saturday. Much of this area has over a 40% chance of \nlow temperatures at or below freezing Friday night according to \nNBM guidance, with as high as a 75% chance in portions of \nNorthern Yuma County. Even so, high temperatures on Saturday are\nforecast to rise above freezing even in the coldest scenarios, \nwhich would suggest a transition into rain if wintry \nprecipitation is able to occur at all Friday night or Saturday \nmorning. There is also the chance that this shortwave system is \nvery weak or not present at all, which represents about 50% of \nthe LREF Grand Ensemble solutions. In this case, drier \nconditions could be expected with downsloping winds from the \nwest that produce warmer temperatures, similar to Friday's \nhighs. Confidence is highest for the latter solution (50-60%), \nwhile there is only about 10% confidence in the solution with \nwintry precipitation. \n\nDeterministic guidance begins to diverge going into Saturday \nnight and Sunday. Looking at ensembles, upper northwesterly flow\nis favored to last as late as Sunday night before a bit more \nzonal flow develops Monday with another shortwave trough. NBM \n24-hr precipitation guidance suggests a 90% chance or greater \nfor no precipitation Sunday through the end of the forecast \nperiod Tuesday evening. This could change on Monday if the \nshortwave trough can deepen a bit similar to the current 00Z \ndeterministic GFS. Highs continue to be forecast in the upper \n40s and 50s Sunday through Tuesday, at or a little above average\nfor this time of year. However, these highs may be highly \nvariable depending on how the atmosphere is modified by the \nnorthwesterly flow pattern earlier in the period.",
